1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/206794877/lloyd-wesley-kamplain
2 CONT
2 CONT Lloyd “Wesley” Kamplain, 72, of Danville, Illinois, passed away at 5:15 p.m. Friday, January 31, 2020 at Carle Hospital in Urbana, Illinois.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born on July 8, 1947 in Champaign, Illinois, the son of George Lloyd and Hazel Olive (Hanson) Kamplain.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wes grew up in the Ogden, Illinois area. He was a true farm boy helping his father in the fields and with livestock, and favorite bull Rocky that his mother referred to as “the animal”. He attended Fithian Elementary and Oakwood T
2 CONC ownship High School where he participated in Future Farmers of America and Student Council and earned an outstanding award for public speaking. He graduated May 26, 1965.
2 CONT
2 CONT Drafted into the U.S. Army September 22, 1966 in Chicago, Illinois, he was ranked Military Policemen and Detectives June 12, 1967. He was decorated with a National Defense Service Medal, Driver/Mechanic Badge with wheeled vehicles
2 CONC  bar, and an expert pistol shot. He served at Fort Hood, TX and numerous places throughout the U.S. He especially liked to share his experience sleeping in the basement of the Pentagon, marching through Arlington Cemetery, and pat
2 CONC rolling during protests. He was honorably discharged September 21, 1972.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wes de-tasseled corn, baled hay and worked for Leverette Bleacher Company where he learned to weld as a teen. That experience brought him to work at Hyster August 19, 1965 with a brief absence when he was drafted to the Army, but 
2 CONC returned to serve as welder, inspector, and receiving clerk before retiring in 2001. He was a member of the Illinois Independent Lift Truck Builders Union. Wes also worked briefly at Ubid, HERRS, Thyssen Krupp, and Automation Inte
2 CONC rnational before fully retiring. Just for fun, he played a mall Santa in 2015.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wes married Sally Kay Hummer February 22, 1969 in Danville, IL. They enjoyed 35 years together before her passing, having daughter Amy and son Greg. He was proud of her nursing career and sewing abilities, and often played pranks 
2 CONC on her involving Amy in his schemes as partner in crime. He learned at Sally’s prompting to be a master carpenter fashioning many of the wood fixtures in the home, flag cases, and much more. Wes took Greg for many rides in his pri
2 CONC zed truck Black Beauty and they had fun talking on the CB being boys doing what boys will do, meaning being ornery!
2 CONT
2 CONT He greatly enjoyed motorcycles, car shows, International Harvester tractors, and Chevy cars and trucks. Wes could always be counted on for jokes and trivia. He could tell you why we drive on the right side of the road, mount a hor
2 CONC se from the left, or how many more snows we would have that are deep enough to track a deer. He borrowed from his dad in saying “what you do and what you say will in turn come back to you some day”. Or, ask you which U.S. Presiden
2 CONC ts are not buried inside the United States? Carter, Bush, Clinton, Obama and Trump! He loved his fur babies too, Rocky, Buster “Bear”, Peaches “Little One”, Butters, Boo and Button
2 CONT
2 CONT A faithful follower of Christ, Wes was baptized September 14, 1947 and enjoyed participating in church functions and bible study. It was at Faith Church of Christ that he married Cathy Liggett July 29, 2017, adopting daughter Sama
2 CONC ntha June 11, 2018. Wes enjoyed the opportunity to be a devoted husband and father again, drawing from his life experiences to provide guidance, wisdom, spiritual leadership to his home and love beyond measure. He was saddened tha
2 CONC t his illness would cut short the time he had to share and his absence is deeply felt. Sammi will miss painting his face with Blue Devils war paint for the homecoming game and having him escort her to the door for the dances she a
2 CONC ttends, as well as melting his heart with a smile and brown eyed pouty face. He loved Cathy’s home cooked meals, especially chicken and noodles, working with her on the serenity rose garden, and walks in the cool evening watching 
2 CONC the sunset and talking about the day and plans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Wes is survived by his wife, Cathy; daughters, Amy Kamplain and Samantha Kamplain all of Danville; son, Greg (Deborah) Kamplain of Crown Point, Indiana; sister, Kathi Wilson of Grand Junction, Colorado; brother, Gary (Mary) Kampla
2 CONC in of Hudson, Illinois; grandson, Daniel and nieces, nephews, great niece and nephew, several cousins and his Uncle John.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was preceded in death by his parents; wife Sally; brother-in-law Ron Wilson and several aunts, uncles and cousins.
2 CONT
2 CONT Burial will be in Danville National Cemetery with Military Rites accorded by American Legion Post 210.
2 CONT
2 CONT News-Gazette, The (Champaign-Urbana, IL)
