1 NAME Johannes Larsen /Franzsen/
2 GIVN Johannes Larsen
2 SURN Franzsen
2 _MARNM Franzsen
1 SEX M
1 BAPM
2 DATE 24 FEB 1850
2 ADDR
3 STAE Rogaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 FEB 1850
2 PLAC Klovning
2 ADDR
3 STAE Rogaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 FEB 1918
2 ADDR
3 CITY Knysna
3 STAE Western Cape
3 CTRY South Africa
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Johannes emigrerte sammen med sin bror Frans (kalte seg Frank i Sør afrika) til Knysna i Sør-Afrika.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born in Tysvær, moved with his parents to Stavanger in 1858, - 8 years old.
2 CONT
2 CONT After his confirmation in Stavanger, Johannes accepted a position in a business in Haugesund. He met the Captain of the Thesen`s scooner, the Albatross and when they decided to emigrate to New Zealand, he offered to be ship`s cook
2 CONC  and landed up with the Thesens on the Albatross which was en-route to New Zealand.
2 CONT
2 CONT They sailed via England on their 78 day voyage to South Africa where they encountered a storm at Cape Agulhas. This necessitated that they had to turn back to Cape Town. On the advice of the Norwegian Consul, they shipped provisio
2 CONC ns to Knysna along the coast where there had been a devastating fire earlier in 1869 in order to earn extra money to repair their boat and to continue their journey. On their arrival in Knysna, the place made such an impression on
2 CONC  them that they decided to settle there in april 1870.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes and Ragnvald Thesen became firm friends on the voyage to South Africa with the result that Johannes eventually stayed on as clerk in the new business started up by the Thesen brothers. Johannes and three of the Thesen bro
2 CONC thers became involved in diamond prospecting in Kimberley and Delportshoop. This however did not last long and Johannes and Ragnvald started a business in Suurvlakte now known as Rheenendal.
2 CONT
2 CONT It was here where Johannes met a very beautiful Soth African girl by the name of Petronella Johanna Zeelie. Johannes`s relationship with Petronella was not approved by her parents because they felt that he was a foreigner. The cou
2 CONC ple were so in love that they continued their relationship and she gave birth to a son. Petronella died in childbirth and her parents, Ernst Johannes Zeelie and Petronella van Wyngaard raised the boy with substancial financial ass
2 CONC istance from Johannes. The boy`s name was Petrus Jacobus Johannes Franzsen, the first Franzsen to be born in South Africa. He was very reluctant to speak about his father Johannes and any questions or attempts at discussion were b
2 CONC rushed aside. According to his grandson Pieter who mentioned that it seemed that Johannes was never allowed to have any contact with his son. It is propably true to say that Johannes was blamed for the death of the boy`s mother by
2 CONC  the grandparents who had forbidden the relationship. Even in old age, his son Petrus would never speak about his father. There is a bible in the family which Johannes had given his son and which is inscribed by him to his son. We
2 CONC ther Johannes had given it to him personally or not is not known.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1875 Johannes resigned from the partnership with Ragnvald and started his own business in Belvidere. He later met and married a very beautiful young lady, Sarah Susanna Hildebrand. His parents were to have arrived from Norway v
2 CONC ia Cape Town in time to attend the wedding but unfortunately the ship they were in was unable to enter the harbour through the heads and they had to wait for a smaller vessel to bring them through which meant they arrived just in 
2 CONC time to see the couple leave on honeymoon. This would have been another source of dissapointment for Johannes.
2 CONT
2 CONT After their arrival from Norway, the parents of Johannes and Frank were visiting them in Belvidere when their mother, Malene went for a walk and was attacked by a bull. She died from her injuries.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1879 Johannes hired a farm which he named Quarrywoods from Mr Palmer. He and his brother, Frank, went into business together but things did not work out and the business was liquidated on 20th May 1886.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johannes started from scratch. He and his family stayed at Modderhoek now known as Brackenhill where he was manager of Thesen`s saw mills. He was appointed as an employee in the forestry department at Ysternek and later Buffelsnek
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT The children attended school at Kruisrivier where his daughter, Constance eventually became a teacher.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is also propably true to say that Johannes was an unhappy man. Firstly because he mourned the death of his first love Petronella, then he was not allowed any access to his son Petrus by the grandparents and also because there w
2 CONC as some animosity between him and his brother Frank`s family possibly because of the child he fathered out of wedlock and which in those days was looked upon with scorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT During March 1897 he decided after selling all his belongings by auction, to leave Knysna and move to Cape Town where he bought a house which he named "Stavanger" in 34 Rosmead Avenue, Gardens, Cape Town.
2 CONT
2 CONT Whilst in Cape Town, amongst other things he was an agent for his nephew Olaf Franzsen, the son of his cousin, Johannes. They were general dealers and direct importers of wood. He was also involved with the supply of equipment for
2 CONC  the alluvial diamond digging industry through a Mr Delport whom he met during the time he spent in Kimberley.
