1 NAME Albrigt Jakobsen /Askeland/
2 GIVN Albrigt Jakobsen
2 SURN Askeland
2 _MARNM Askeland
2 NICK Økland
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 27 DEC 1830
2 PLAC Økland
2 ADDR
3 CITY Bremnes
3 STAE Hordaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 BAPM
2 DATE 18 JAN 1831
2 PLAC Finnås
2 ADDR
3 STAE Hordaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 DEAT
2 DATE 6 NOV 1893
2 PLAC Søra Askeland br. 3
2 ADDR
3 CITY Tysværvåg
3 STAE Rogaland
3 CTRY Norway
1 BURI
2 DATE 11 NOV 1893
1 OCCU Gårdbruker
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Gaardbruger, selveier. Askeland Søndre - løpenr/bnr 131. Han fekk skøyte i 1859. I 1875 fødde dei hest, stut, 7 kyr, 23 sauer og 3 geiter. Dei sådde 5 tønner havre og 5 tønner poteter. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Info taken from Tysvaer Bygdebok #2, page 175, #27
2 CONT
2 CONT Moved to Økland in Bremnes, moved to Tysvær in 1836 lived farm # 3, Søre Askeland, Tysvær, Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Other names of children of Albrigt and Ingeborg were Anne Dorthea, Ester Lovise, Elen Sofie, Jakob, Lisbet Kirstine, Karen Martea, Martin, Ingeborg Agrethe and Laurits. Of these children Ray, Martin and Laurits came to USA. Nothin
2 CONC g is known about Martin. Some think Martin may have died in the San Francisco earth quake. Laurits lived and died in Illinois. Karen Martea died young. The rest stayed in Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT Russ, People were always buried on the local church's cemetery. If they belonged to the �rdal church, they also were buried there. Never on a farm.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Hjelmeland people were buried on the cemetery at Hjelmeland church. Quite a long way to take the body, but sure they did. Then one week after the funeral, a big party was held, a so called 'gravøl'. All the neighbors were invi
2 CONC ted, and it was a duty to participate. A gravøl was not necessarily a sad party, on the contrary the meaning was to be lively and have fun, so that the soul of the deceased would better get peace and rest -. Arne
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage in Norway
2 CONT
2 CONT Russ,
2 CONT
2 CONT What I know about marriages in the past is that people always married in a church, the one they belonged to. You had to pay a consumption fee to get married, but an exception for farmers and other common people. For them the weddi
2 CONC ng was free of charge. So, from 1863 people were allowed to marry without the cooperation of the church, and could go to the judge to have a civil marriage. As far as I know, you had to pay a fee then.
2 CONT
2 CONT About baptism.
2 CONT
2 CONT Newborn babies, looking weak and sickly, were christened at home just after the birth, often by the father. Then the baptism was confirmed in the church some weeks later. There are often notations in the church records like: 'The 
2 CONC child died before the baptism was confirmed in the church.' Arne
2 CONT
2 CONT Census Notes
2 CONT
2 CONT 1865 Norwegain census Albrigt Jakobs. Husfader Gaardbruger, Selveier g 35 m Finnaas Prgj. 1 Engebor Rasmusd. hans Kone g 32 k Finnaas Prgj. Anne Albrigtsd. deres Datter ug 12 k Tysvaer Prgj. Ester L Albrigtsd. deres Datter ug 10 k
2 CONC  Tysvaer Prgj. Soffie Albrigtsd. deres Datter Ê ug 7 k Tysvaer Prgj. Jakob Albrigtss. deres Son ug 5 m Tysvaer Prgj. Lisebet K. Albrigtsd. deres Datter Ê ug 3 k Tysvaer Prgj.
2 CONT
2 CONT Folketelling fra 1865: http://digitalarkivet.arkivverket.no/ft/person/pf01038217002114
2 CONT
