2 CONT 1920 Federal Census, Rutland Twp, Humboldt County, IA Askland, Ray, age 52, immigrated 1886, US citizen 1894 Askland, Hanna, age 45, immigrated 1894, US citizen 1894 Askland, Henry, son, age 24 Askland, Ray Jr., son, age 16 Asklan
2 CONC d, Arthur, son, age 14 Askland, Alice, dau, age 12 Askland, Charlie, son, age 9 Askland, Mildred, dau, age 6 Askland, Esther, dau, age 1 Rosedal, Helmer, nephew, age 1
2 CONT
2 CONT 1930 Federal Census, Rutland Twp, Humboldt County, IA Askland, Ray, head, age 63, immigrated 1887, not US citizen Askland, Hanna, wife, age 55, immigrated 1893, not US citizen Askland, Arthur, son, age 25 Askland, Charlie, son, ag
2 CONC e 20 Askland, Mildred, dau, age 17 Askland, Esther, dau, age 15 Rosedal, Helmer, grandson, age 11
2 CONT
2 CONT Reinart Albrigtsen (Ray Askland) Was the seventh of ten children of Albrigt Jakobsen (Askeland) and Ingeborg Rasmusdtr. Albrigt and Ingeborg were lst cousins. Nothing is known about ReinartÕs life in Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT info from Art Askland Reinart was about 5Õ2ÕÕ in height. He weighted about 165 lbs. He had huge hands for a man of his size. He wore a size 38 coat. He chewed tobacco and loved to smoke his pipe. Ray would not eat any food that wa
2 CONC s red in color he said Òthe devil made it redÓ. He called beet Òdevil beets. One of RayÕs favorite food was dried beef. Ray loved to go fishing. He also loved to have a beer on an occasion. Hanna had a son before the marriage to R
2 CONC ay his name was Henry. Hanna never married the father. In the year 1917 the Askland family moved to Humboldt, Iowa. They purchased a farm 4 miles North of Humboldt, Iowa on March 17, 1917. They paid Forty-six thousand five hundred
2 CONC  dollars for the farm. The farm was the Southwest Quarter and West half of the Southeast quarter of section Thirteen in Township ninety-two North Range twenty-nine. In the year of 1928 Ray and Hanna lost there farm. They were unab
2 CONC le to pay a tax for a drainage ditch that was put in near there farm. Ray and Hanna moved to the town of Humboldt, Iowa. Ray was very religious, attended church every Sunday and rang the bell each Sunday for church.
2 CONT
2 CONT Some of my Memories of Hanna and Reinert Askland Bette Christensen Nichols My memory of Grandma Askland is much stronger than of Grandpa Askland. I feel she was the dominant figure. Grandma always looked the same to me all through
2 CONC  the years. A pleasant lady with many lines in her face. I am sure brought on by many hours of work in the garden caring for her chickens, etc. She had long, white hair which she wound into a bun during the day and braided at nigh
2 CONC t, letting the braid hang down her back. She used tortoise shell hairpins to hold the bun in place. In later years she had her hair cut and went to the beauty shop across the street from her house to have it set. She had a sturdy 
2 CONC body, but never too heavy. she had lean upper arms and thighs, not flabby like many older people. She wore round glasses with metal frames. When home, she wore print dresses and aprons and sturdy black shoes. When going out, she w
2 CONC ore dresses made of silk-type fabrics with a pin at the neckline and often a necklace also. She also wore a hat when dressing up.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa Askland was a mild-mannered man with graying, thinning hair. He was a rather small man. He had a mustache which he shaved off once and Grandma wouldn't speak to him. He tried to get back in her good graces by drying dishes
2 CONC , etc. He smoked a pipe. In nice weather he sat in a chair in the back yard and smoked his pipe, but in bad weather he smoked in the basement. He did yard work for people and pushed a red hand cart. He did work for Pastor Anderson
2 CONC . The Anderson's lived across the street from the tan house, directly north. Grandpa would buy ice cream cones for me. He drank coffee out of a saucer. He wore elastic bands (like garters) to hold his shirt sleeves up so they woul
2 CONC dn't hang over his hands. He wore leather bedroom slippers.
2 CONT
2 CONT I remember Grandpa getting angry with me only once. They lived in the house a block south and a half block east of the Lutheran Church (the old one). I told Shari there was a ball under the table in the pantry, knowing there was a
2 CONC  mousetrap under the table and not a ball. She went under the table to get it and the trap went off. I don't think she was hurt, but Grandpa gave me a swat on the behind. I was devastated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandpa and Grandpa lived in five houses in Humboldt, to my knowledge. My earliest memory is of them living in the large white house on the corner across from the park by the river (where the bandstand is). It was on the northeast
2 CONC  corner from the park. I was very young when they lived there. Grandma took care of me while my mother worked at the bank. I remember falling down the stairs in this house. Prior to this house, they probably lived in the house two
2 CONC  houses west of the last house they lived in (the tan house with brown trim). There is a picture of my mother (Mildred) and me in front of this house. My mother is dressed in a flapper style with bobbed hair and I am very young an
2 CONC d blond.
2 CONT
2 CONT The second house I remember was not too far from the fairgrounds. It is the house where the picture was taken of me in snowsuit and cap. I was probably five years old. The house where I picked a little hard green grape from a vine
2 CONC  and put in my nose and couldn't get it out. Why, I have no idea. Just being a kid. The house Alice was giving a shower and had me drive the gifts in on a pedal car. I believe the car was borrowed from the Jaqua family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The next house was the house a block south and a half block east of the old Lutheran Church, between the Naugal (sp?) and the Shellenburger (sp?) houses. Newsomes (sp?) lived close by and had goats. I remember being sick at Christ
2 CONC mas time and having to stay in bed while everyone was eating and having fun. Alice had my 6th or 7th birthday party in this house. I think everyone in my class was invited. It was a memorable party. I remember that the Christmas t
2 CONC ree caught on fire and did some damage to the house and furniture. Grandpa and Grandma still lived in this house when we moved to Des Moines.
2 CONT
2 CONT From there they moved to the tan house about 1941 or 1942. This is the house where I stayed many summers visiting Grandma and Grandpa. Also, Christmas time. Sometimes I was put on the bus in Des Moines, transferred in Fort Dodge a
2 CONC nd then a bus to Humboldt. Not a good idea in this day and age. We also took the train at times, the M&ST.L (Minneapolis and St. Louis Railroad line) which we could take from Des Moines to Humboldt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Of course, Aunt Alice lived at home until she married Charlie. Sometimes I would walk to the bank and then we would walk to Charlie’'s gas station, pick up his car and drive home for lunch. Alice always came home for lunch. Charli
2 CONC e and Alice would occasionally take me shopping in Fort Dodge and then eat at Treloar’s Restaurant which was a special treat. They were known for their fried chicken.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma always had a Christmas tree that reached to the ceiling. This was very impressive to me because we always had a little one that sat on a table. I always loved their Christmas trees.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma did so much sewing, crocheting, knitting and quilting. She made pot holders, aprons, mittens for everyone. She knitted mittens for all the grandchildren with a crocheted string that went through the coat sleeves so they wo
2 CONC uldn't get lost. She used every scrap of yarn, crochet thread, material. Sometimes the combinations were pretty wild because she did make use of every scrap.
2 CONT
2 CONT We played Chinese checkers and Rummy, but never on Sunday. She didn't do knitting or crocheting, etc. on Sunday. On Sunday they went to church. In later years, Grandpa preferred to stay home and listen to the Norwegian service on 
2 CONC the radio. I believe it emanated from Northwood, Minnesota. In earlier years they had a Norwegian service at the church and then they were discontinued.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma used a rubber mat to lean her knees on when she washed the floors.
2 CONT
2 CONT I loved going to Weirs Grocery Store for her. It was a little grocery store in Weir's home, not far from where she lived in the tan and brown house. She would give us a coin purse and a list and send us to the grocery store. She w
2 CONC ould let us spend money to buy candy. In retrospect, I realize she could't afford this. We were so poor, it seemed everyone else was rich.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma would always do her baking early in the morning, before anyone was else was up. She made little girl cookies with pink icing, spritz, kringla, lefse, potato cakes and lots of other good things. The other things I liked to 
2 CONC eat at her house were rusk toast, Zwieback toast, wieners and sauerkraut or ring bologna with boiled potatoes, pickled wax beans (which she canned herself), maraschino cherries. One time she let me eat a whole jar of maraschino ch
2 CONC erries. They always had their main meal at noon and a lighter meal in the evening. They had a nice storeroom in the basement where she kept the canned goods she made and extra items she bought at the store. Another thing she made 
2 CONC was look-aid popsicles made in ice cube trays with toothpicks for handles.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma and Grandpa would have a snack mid-morning, mid-afternoon and before going to bed.
2 CONT
2 CONT I loved going to Ladies Aid at the church with Grandma. It seemed they usually had tuna sandwiches and coffee. I also loved going shopping in Fort Dodge with her. We would take the bus.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Grandma broke her hip, she always said it broke before she hit the floor. She was told she would never walk again but she did. She had a three-legged cane and she practiced walking around the kitchen table. After this happene
2 CONC d, at a later date, she tore down the old chicken house in the backyard .
2 CONT
2 CONT At one time they had a fish pond in the back yard with gold fish.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma would let us play house in the yard in the summer under the shade of the trees on the east of the garden. There was a nice lawn with a line of trees. We would carry all kinds of furniture and junk out of the basement (tabl
2 CONC es, chairs, etc.) and when we were through we would carry it all back down the basement. We would have kool-aid and cookies.
2 CONT
2 CONT I remember Grandma packing her square wicker basket and going to the park on picnics. I remember walking across the river on the rocks. I think Grandma must have been a very energetic woman.
2 CONT
2 CONT She told me that once I arrived at her house all by myself. She asked how I got there and I told her “I came by my lone. I do not recall this. Another incident she told me about was when I wanted a wrapped sandwich. She tried ever
2 CONC ything, including wrapping it in waxpaper, but nothing satisfied me. She discovered later that I wanted the bread folded without being cut, which is what I had seen my father do.
2 CONT
2 CONT She always said she never wanted to go to a nursing home. I am very sorry she had to spend her last years in a nursing home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandma and Grandpa were very special people to me. They gave me a feeling of stability that I did not have in my own home.
2 CONT
2 CONT Grandmother Hanna
2 CONT
2 CONT Joann I do remember that Grandpa Askland had to smoke his pipe in the basement, and I could never understand why. I don't remember him ever saying anything. He was real quiet and Grandma Askland was always talking in Norwegian. I 
2 CONC also remember being fascinated with Grandma Askland's tongue. It looked like it had a deep grove in it right down the middle. I always wondered if she cut it somehow. There were so many grandchildren around whenever we were at a f
2 CONC amily gathering that I think we all got lost in the shuffle with the exception of Donna. She was one of the first grandchildren, I think. She even lived with Grandma Askland so she could graduate from high school in Humboldt.
2 CONT
2 CONT xmas by Inez My reply is not only grandma and grandpa liked kringla with butter, so did the whole family. And lefse with butter and sugar was a must at Christmas, Thanksgiving, New Years and every other Holiday throughout the wint
2 CONC er months. I haven't made kringla for quite a while but when my kids were young and at home kringla was expected. That is good eating. When I was a kid we spent Christmas at grandma and grandpas house. Then on the 26th we had our 
2 CONC Christmas at our house as it was Dad's birthday. So we celebrated birthday and Christmas all at once. Grandma always had fruitcake, (never did like that stuff) She always made all kinds of cookies. When dinner was all the adults a
2 CONC te in the dining room and all the kids ate in the kitchen. When dinner was over the ladies and older girls cleaned up the tables and done the dishes. The smaller kids played outside, weather permitting.The men went either outside 
2 CONC or to the basement to visit and smoke THAT WAS THE GOOD OLD DAYS!!!!!!!!!!!
2 CONT
2 CONT obit: Ray Askland was born in Tysvaer, near Stavanger, Norway February 1, 167 to Albert and Engeborg Askeland and passed away at his home in Humboldt, IA, Friday evening September 5, 1847 at the age of 80 years, 7 months and five 
2 CONC days. Ray Askland grew to manhood in Norway, where he was baptized and confirmed in the Lutheran faith. At the time of his death he was a member of the First Lutheran Church of Humboldt, IA He came to USA in the year 1887 and loca
2 CONC ted near Rochelle, IL. In 1917 he and his family moved to a farm north of Humboldt, which was his home until he retired from farming in 1831 and moved in the city of Humboldt. On the 21 day of January 1895, Ray Askland was united 
2 CONC in marriage with Hanna Ramsdal at Dixon, IL. To this union was born nine children, five girls and four boys, eight of which are still living; Henry of Garner; Ray Jr of Fort Dodge: Arthur of Fort Dodge; Charlie of Thor, IA; Edna, 
2 CONC Mrs Ed Askeland of Thor; Alice, Mrs Charles Wendle of Humboldt; Esther, Mrs James Skow of Bode, IA; Mildred, Mrs Mildred Christensen of Des Moines. One daughter Helen, Mrs Chris Rosedal, preceeded him in death. At her passing in 1
2 CONC 919 she left an infant son, Helmer Rosedal, who was also raised in th Birth Notes in english Òs¿re mean southÓAskeland means Òland of the Ash TreesÓ Religion Notes member of 1st Lutheran Church in Humboldt, IA member of Humboldt/C
2 CONC ornith Lutheran Church in 1924 Baptism Notes godparents: Jacob Albrigtsen Asekeland, Knud Ramsussen L¿nning, Ingeborg Jacobsdtr Hervig, Kari Ellingsdtr Hersdal, Inger Christophersdtr Askeland Burial Notes pall bearers were: Helmer
2 CONC  Rosedal, George Askeland,Elmer Askeland,Oscar Askeland,Ben Wooster, John Askeland Birth Citation Tysvaer Bygdebok #2 (Direct & primary evidence) Page: 176 #28g Date: 1867 Text: farm søre Askeland, living with parents, three broth
2 CONC ers and six sisters Name Citation 1910 Federal Census (DeKalb, IL) (Direct & primary evidence) Date: 1910 Text: husband, living with wife Hanna, dau's Edna, Helen, Alice, son's Ray, Arthur, Charles Name Citation 1920 Federal Censu
2 CONC s (Humboldt County, IA) (Direct & primary evidence) Date: 1920 Text: husband, living with wife Hanna, son's Henry, Ray, Arthur, Charlie, grandson, Helmer, dau's Alice, Mildred, Esther Name Citation 1930 Federal Census (Humboldt Co
2 CONC unty, IA) (Direct & primary evidence) Date: 1930 Text: husband, living with wife Hanna, son's Arthur, Charlie, dau's Mildred, Esther, grandson Helmer Baptism Citation Tysvaer Church (Tysvaer Norway) Date: Apr 07 1867 Text: church 
2 CONC records Death Citation Tombstone Date: 1947 Text: Union Cemetery, Humboldt, IIA Death Citation Obituary (Direct & primary evidence) Date: Sep 05 1947 Text: Humboldt, IA newspaper Marriage (m. RAMSDAL, Hanna Ordine) Citation Obitua
