1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Golden Wedding Ed & Edna Askeland 1968 written by Mary Orsland
2 CONT
2 CONT Today we are celebrating Golden Wedding bells with our folks who have lived, loved and planned 50 years together. I am sure that it really doesnÕt seem so long since Dad ventured from Stavanger, Norway to Iowa where he has made hi
2 CONC s home. When he first came to Iowa he worked on a farm owned by Hans & Tena Lenning. TenaÕs cooking was wonderful but there was an Edna who had a flair for baking Kringla and cooking Lutefisk. It wasnÕt long before they decided to
2 CONC  go to Julesburg, Colorado and get married. The honeymoon was over and so back to work.
2 CONT
2 CONT They lived on the North edge of Humboldt where Ed worked on a farm and did some tiling. It was there that Sarah was born. Then they moved Northeast of Thor, IA. Here Ed started farming. Soon after Sarah was born they got a little 
2 CONC sister, Helen. As time went on Elmer joined his sisters. They moved North of Thor where Mary, George, Agnes and Oscar were born. Finally they moved Southwest of Thor. Last, but not least came Inez. It was on this farm we thought o
2 CONC f as home. Living 32 years on the same farm creates a lot of memories. Year after year grain was planted. There were lots of milk cows, hogs and chickens. Then of course Mom cooked, canned and washed clothes for the family. It too
2 CONC k lots of baking because we were such a large family of eight. Life wasnÕt as convenient in those days, but sharing and helping each another certainly was prevalent. There has been much joy and some grief through the years, but no
2 CONC  life can be complete without joy and tears.
2 CONT
2 CONT The time to retire. So Mom and Dad decided to leave the farm. Eight years ago (1960) they had a sale and moved to their present home in Thor. It is nice that our parents are living in a friendly community where they have their fri
2 CONC ends; most of the children are not far away. So truly, dear parents this must be a Golden Day in many ways for you. Golden memories now passing in reviewÉ golden in the promise that along the future way, youÕll find more happiness
2 CONC  to share, together day by day.
2 CONT
2 CONT Obituary Mrs. Edna Albertina Askeland, 79, died Tuesday at the Rotary Ann Home in Eagle Grove where she was a resident for the past 4 1/2 years. Services will be at 2pm Friday in the Thor Lutheran Church with burial in East Ullens
2 CONC vang Cemetery near Thor. Friends may call at Kastler-Bacock-Deyoung Furneral Home in Eagle Grove after 7pm Wednesday.
2 CONT
2 CONT Surviving are sons Elmer Askeland and Oscar Askeland, both of Fort Dodge; daughters, Mrs. Ben Wooster, Eagle Grove; Mrs. Lee Eaton, Denver, Colo, Mrs. Raymond Orsland, Thor, Mrs. Milton Welch, Denver, Colo; Mrs. Ed Edgerton, San D
2 CONC iego, Calif; 26 grandchildren, several ggrandchildren; brothers Henry Askland, Miller, and Arthur, Fort Dodge; sisters Mrs. Earnest Edge and Mrs. Esther Lenning, both of Humboldt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Edna Albertina Askland was born at DeKalb, Ill, and attended schools there. She moved to Humboldt and married Ed Askeland at Julesburg, Colo in 1918. The couple farmed in the Thor, Badger and Humboldt areas, moving to Thor in 1960
2 CONC  when he retired. Mr. Askeland died in 1976.
